sections.text:
Many everyday machines are run by computer control systems:
• sensors measure a condition (temperature, light, movement, moisture);
• a processor compares the reading with a set (target) value and decides;
• actuators make a change (switch a fan, motor, pump or lamp on or off).
The sensor keeps measuring, so the system checks the effect of its own action: this is feedback. Examples: automatic doors, traffic lights, thermostats, greenhouse watering, automatic street lights.
